---
title: 'Recruiting Metrics for Startups: Time to Hire, Funnel Conversion and Source of Hire'
publishedAt: 2026-11-11
type: companies
---
**Quick answer:** Startups only need a handful of recruiting metrics: **time to hire** (days from application to accepted offer), **stage conversion rates** (the share of candidates who move from each stage to the next), **time in stage** (where candidates get stuck), **source of hire** (which channels produce hires, not just applicants), **offer acceptance rate**, and **cost per hire**. Track them per job, look at them every week or two, and fix the biggest bottleneck first. A slow stage or a channel that brings applicants but never hires tells you more than any headline number.

Big companies have recruiting operations teams and dashboards. Startups usually have a founder, a spreadsheet and a feeling that hiring is "slow". Measuring a few simple numbers turns that feeling into something you can fix.

## Why should startups track recruiting metrics?

- **Strong candidates don't wait.** Good engineers often have several processes running at once, so a slow process loses them.
- **Founders' time is limited.** Metrics show where hours are being wasted.
- **Channels vary hugely.** Some sources bring hundreds of applicants and no hires.
- **You can't fix what you can't see.** "Hiring is slow" becomes "candidates wait nine days between the take-home and the review".

## The 6 recruiting metrics that matter

{% table %}
- Metric
- What it measures
- How to calculate
---
- **Time to hire**
- Speed of your process
- Days from application (or first contact) to accepted offer
---
- **Stage conversion rate**
- Where candidates drop out
- Candidates reaching stage B ÷ candidates who reached stage A
---
- **Time in stage**
- Where the process stalls
- Median days candidates spend in each stage
---
- **Source of hire**
- Which channels work
- Hires (and interviews) by source
---
- **Offer acceptance rate**
- How competitive your offers are
- Offers accepted ÷ offers made
---
- **Cost per hire**
- Efficiency
- (Tool, ad and agency costs + team time) ÷ hires
{% /table %}

### 1. Time to hire

Time to hire runs from when a candidate enters your process to when they accept an offer. A related metric, **time to fill**, runs from when the role opens to when it's filled.

Time to hire tells you how fast your process is for a candidate. Time to fill tells you how long the business goes without the person.

There's no universal benchmark. It depends on role, seniority and market. Focus on improving your own number over time, and compare roles inside your company.

### 2. Stage conversion rates

Conversion shows where candidates leave your funnel. A typical engineering funnel:

**Applied → Screening → Technical → Final → Offer → Hired**

What to look for:

- **Very low conversion from Applied to Screening** often means your job post attracts the wrong people. See our guide to [writing better job descriptions](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-write-a-software-engineer-job-description-that-attracts-the-right-applicants-template).
- **Low conversion from Technical to Final** may mean your screening isn't filtering well, or your assessment is too hard or unrelated to the job.
- **Many candidates withdrawing** at any stage suggests the process is too long or the candidate experience is poor.

### 3. Time in stage

This is often the most useful metric for a startup, because it points to an exact fix. If candidates spend a median of eight days in "Take-home review", someone needs to review faster.

Watch for applications that sit in the first stage for days with nothing happening. These are the candidates most likely to give up on you.

### 4. Source of hire

Track where each candidate came from: job boards, your careers page, referrals, outreach, agencies, events.

The key insight: **measure hires and interviews by source, not just applications.** A job board that sends 300 applicants and zero interviews is costing you screening time, not helping.

### 5. Offer acceptance rate

If many candidates decline your offers, look at:

- Compensation versus market.
- Speed: did a competitor move faster?
- Candidate experience during interviews.
- Whether expectations on remote work, scope or title were set early.

Ask candidates who decline why. Most will tell you.

### 6. Cost per hire

Include tools, job ads, agency fees and a rough estimate of interviewer time. For startups, team time is usually the biggest hidden cost. A founder spending 15 hours per hire on first-round screens is expensive.

## Metrics most startups don't need yet

- **Quality of hire**: important, but hard to measure until you've made enough hires and run performance reviews. Start with a simple check-in at 90 days.
- **Diversity funnel metrics by demographic**: valuable, but they require collecting data carefully and lawfully. If you track this, keep it separate from hiring decisions.
- **Complex recruiter productivity metrics**: useful at scale, overkill for a team of ten.

## How to improve each metric

{% table %}
- Problem
- Likely fix
---
- Slow time to hire
- Cut rounds, schedule interviews back to back, set review deadlines
---
- Candidates stuck in the first stage
- Daily triage, AI screening to sort by fit, a "needs attention" check
---
- Low Applied → Screening conversion
- Clearer job post, salary range, knockout questions for hard requirements
---
- High withdrawals mid-process
- Shorter assessments, faster feedback, regular updates
---
- Low offer acceptance
- Market-rate offers, move faster, sell the role throughout
---
- Sources with no hires
- Stop paying for them; invest in referrals and outreach
{% /table %}

